  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level5_MacLab
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_5
  levelNumber: 5
  displayName: The Compromised Campus Network
  sceneName: Room5_MacLab
  description: Suspicious messages have been reported in the university MacLab. Investigate
    the flagged workstations and decide what's real.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ec2bc026c46543c4788ea2715af04007, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 5: THE COMPROMISED CAMPUS NETWORK'
  briefingText: 'The campus network has been compromised. This room has six questions:
    four case questions with four answer choices and two messages or web pages to
    judge as SCAM or LEGITIMATE. They arrive in a different order on every attempt,
    and the answer choices are shuffled too, so read each one carefully instead of
    remembering positions.'
  caseNotes:
  - A "Scholarship Grant 2027" form is shared in a student group. It uses the school
    logo.
  - The canteen's "Scan to pay with PayLite" poster looks normal, but up close the
    QR code is a sticker placed on top of the printed one.
  objectives:
  - Answer all six questions in this room.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose.
  - Score 1080 or more to complete the investigation.
  messages:
  - id: H05
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: A friend was scammed through a chat app and wants to file a report. <b>Which
      way of handling the evidence is best?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Evidence has to be <b>complete</b> and <b>unchanged</b> to be trusted.
      Cropping removes context, and blurring removes the scammer's identifiers that
      investigators need. Keeping the original device and noting how you collected
      each item is a basic <b>chain of custody</b>. Uninstalling may delete the chat
      history. Group chats spread personal data.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Crop the screenshots to just the scam message and blur the scammer's name for
      privacy
    - Forward everything to a big group chat so more people can confirm it
    - Keep the full conversation untouched on the original phone. Take full screenshots
      showing the sender's profile or number with dates and times, save receipts
      with reference numbers, share only copies, and note when and how each item
      was collected
    - Uninstall the app to stop the scammer and write down what you remember
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: Keep the <b>originals untouched</b>, share copies, and write down when
      you saved each one.
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Legal Aspects of Cyber Forensics
      (\"maintaining evidence integrity\"). Supporting: Digital Evidence and Evidence
      Collection"
  - id: H06
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: 'Email header excerpt:

      From: "Registrar''s Office" <noparse><</noparse>registrar@uphsl-binan.example<noparse>></noparse>

      Reply-To:
      registrar.office.ph@freemail.example

      Return-Path: <noparse><</noparse>bounce@mailer-xyz.example<noparse>></noparse>

      Authentication-Results:
      spf=fail; dkim=none; dmarc=fail

      Subject: FINAL reminder: unpaid tuition,
      account on hold

      <b>What is the strongest evidence that this is spoofed?</b>'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The "From" line can say anything, so the display name proves nothing.
      <b>SPF/DMARC fail</b> means the registrar's domain did not authorize the server
      that sent this email. The <b>Reply-To</b> quietly sends your answer to the
      attacker. The subject and time are only weak signs.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - SPF and DMARC fail for the registrar's domain, and replies are redirected to
      a free-mail address
    - The subject mentions tuition
    - The display name says "Registrar's Office"
    - It was sent at 2:14 AM
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: SPF and DMARC <b>failed</b>, and replies go to free-mail. The 'From'
      name is just text anyone can type.
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Network Forensics and Cybercrime
      Investigation (\"identify signs of cybercrimes\")"
  - id: H07
    channel: 2
    sender: Scholarship Grant 2027 form
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scholarship Grant 2027
    body: 'Please provide: full name, student number, birthdate, mother''s maiden
      name, front and back of your ID, a selfie holding your ID, and your PayLite
      MPIN "for fast release of funds".


      There is no privacy notice. The only
      contact is a mobile number.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: A "Scholarship Grant 2027" form is shared in a student group.
      It uses the school logo.
    explanation: 'Real scholarship offices sometimes do ask for ID, which is what
      makes this hard. The deciding signs are: <b>(1)</b> no one ever needs your
      MPIN to <i>send</i> you money; <b>(2)</b> an ID plus a selfie holding it is
      exactly what''s needed to open accounts in your name; <b>(3)</b> there is no
      privacy notice and no official contact. That breaks the DPA principles of <b>transparency</b>
      and <b>proportionality</b>. Check through the scholarship office''s official
      channel.'
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Scam. <b>No one needs your MPIN to pay you.</b> With ID + selfie +
      MPIN, they can become you.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Week 15: Data
      Privacy Act (RA 10173)"
  - id: H08
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: After you open an email attachment on a lab PC, your files start changing
      to <b>.locked</b> and a note demands cryptocurrency. <b>What do you do first?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'First, <b>contain</b> it: cutting the network connection stops
      it spreading to shared drives and other PCs. Leaving the PC on keeps evidence
      in memory for investigators. Follow your organization''s response plan if it
      says otherwise. Paying doesn''t guarantee you''ll get your files back. Restarting
      or cleaning can destroy evidence. Copying to a USB can spread the infection
      or copy already-locked files.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Pay quickly before the price goes up
    - Disconnect the PC from the network (unplug the cable or turn off Wi-Fi), leave
      it on, stop using it, and report to IT or the incident team immediately
    - Restart, run a cleanup tool and delete the ransom note
    - Copy your files to your USB drive to save them
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: <b>Disconnect, don't shut down, report.</b> Containment first; investigators
      need the evidence.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Incident Response and Forensics"
  - id: H09
    channel: 2
    sender: Page opened by the canteen QR code
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scan to pay with PayLite
    body: Enter your mobile number and MPIN to pay.
    link: paylite-merchant-pay.example/canteen
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: The canteen's "Scan to pay with PayLite" poster looks normal,
      but up close the QR code is a sticker placed on top of the printed one.
    explanation: This is QR code phishing. A sticker over the original is the physical
      clue. A real merchant QR opens <b>inside the e-wallet app</b> and shows the
      merchant's name for you to confirm. It never asks for your MPIN on a web page.
      Also tell the canteen staff, so the next person doesn't get caught.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Scam. Sticker over sticker, a browser page and an MPIN request. Real
      payments happen <b>inside the app</b>.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 17:
      Real-World Applications and Case Studies"
  - id: H10
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: You find a fake school login page. Its admin page is publicly open and
      shows a list of <b>student passwords</b> it has captured. <b>What is the ethical
      action?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The ACM Code of Ethics asks computing professionals to <b>avoid
      harm (1.2)</b>, <b>respect privacy (1.6)</b> and <b>access computing resources
      only when authorized (2.8)</b>. Downloading the list means handling other people's
      stolen data without authorization. Posting the link sends more victims to it.
      Testing it hands over your own password. Reporting lets the people with authority
      act.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Download the list and message each victim yourself
    - Post the link on the freedom wall to warn everyone
    - Test it with your own real password to confirm it works
    - Report it right away to the school's IT or security office with screenshots
      and the URL written in a non-clickable form (e.g., hxxp://), without logging
      in or using the captured data
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Report it, don't touch it. Good intentions don't make <b>unauthorized
      access</b> okay.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Week 16: Professional
      Organization and ACM Code of Ethics"
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l5_old_welcome
    channel: 1
    sender: MacLab Coordinator
    senderDetail: labs@uphsl-binan.example
    subject: Welcome to MacLab 3
    body: Welcome to MacLab 3. Please log out when you finish and report anything
      suspicious to security@uphsl-binan.example. Official notices are always posted
      on the student portal.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 
    curriculumRef: 
  caseFiles:
  - title: Case Notes
    body: "\u2022 A \"Scholarship Grant 2027\" form is shared in a student group.
      It uses the school logo.\n\u2022 The canteen's \"Scan to pay with PayLite\"
      poster looks normal, but up close the QR code is a sticker placed on top of
      the printed one."
  - title: How This Room Works
    body: 'Six questions are waiting in this room. They arrive one at a time, in
      a different order on every attempt.


      Case questions have four answer
      choices, shuffled each attempt. Pick the safest and most accurate one.


      Messages
      and web pages are judged as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.


      Score 1080 or more
      to complete the investigation.'
  firstMessageDelay: 8
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 8, y: 14}
  minimumActivityDelay: 4
  scoring:
    basePoints: 300
    speedBonus: 30
    fastWindow: 0.35
    wrongPenalty: 60
    recoveryPoints: 70
    baseReadingSeconds: 20
    secondsPerWord: 0.5
    secondsPerDetail: 3
    minReadingSeconds: 40
    maxReadingSeconds: 120
    caseExtraSeconds: 75
    overtimeStepSeconds: 10
    overtimePenaltyPerStep: 10
    maxTimePenalty: 70
    minCorrectPoints: 230
  oneStarScore: 720
  twoStarScore: 1080
  threeStarScore: 1800
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 1080
  nextLevelId: 
  nextSceneName: 
  lessons:
  - Keep evidence complete, unchanged and documented.
  - Use technical header evidence rather than what the email shows on the surface.
  - Separate normal data requests from identity-theft collection using DPA principles.
  - 'Know the first response steps: contain, preserve evidence, report.'
  - 'Treat QR codes like links: check where they lead, and look for physical tampering.'
  - Respond to discovered wrongdoing within your authority and without causing more
    harm.
  learningSummary: 
  performanceHigh: Outstanding! The campus network is secure - every flagged message
    was correctly identified.
  performanceModerate: Good investigation. Review the clues you missed - the official
    records separate the real notices from the fakes.
  performanceLow: Keep practising. Compare every sender, attachment and request with
    the university's official records.
